'''anomeomery''' (an-ọ̄-mẹ̄-om′e̤-ri), ''n.'' [< Gr. as if *ἀνομοιομέρεια, < ἀνομοιομερής, consisting of unlike parts, not homogeneous, < ἀν- priv. + ὁμοιομερής, consisting of like parts: see ''an-''<sup>5</sup> and ''homeomery.''] In the ''hist. of phil.'', the Italic form of the doctrine of atoms, which rejected the Anaximandrian principle of homeomery (which see).

