'''anomiid''' (an-ọ̄-mī′id), ''n.'' A bivalve mollusk of the family ''Anomiidæ.''

'''Anomiidæ''' (an-ọ̄-mī′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Anomia'' + ''-idæ.''] A family of asiphonate lamellibranchiate mollusks, typified
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|''Anomia achæus.''<br>''p, p,'' muscular impressions.}}}}
by the genus ''Anomia.'' {{smaller|The typical species have thin, unequal, irregular valves, the flattest of which is deeply notched for the passage of a muscle to a calcareous or chitinous plate by which the shell adheres to other shells.}}

'''anomite''' (an′ọ̄-mīt), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἄνομος, without law (see ''anomo-''), + ''-ite''<sup>2</sup>.] '''1.''' A subspecies of the mica called ''biotite'' (which see), distinguished by certain optical properties.—'''2.''' A fossil of the genus ''Anomites;'' an extinct species of the ''Anomiidæ'' or some similar shell.

'''anomo-.''' [Combining form of Greek ἄνομος, < ἀ- priv. + νόμος, law: see ''nome.''] An element in compound words of Greek origin, meaning irregular, unusual.

'''Anomobranchiata''' (an″ọ̄-mọ̄-brang-ki-ā′tä̤), ''n. pl.'' [NL., neut. pl. of ''anomobranchiatus:'' see ''anomobranchiate.''] A group of crustaceans: synonymous with ''Stomatopoda'' (which see). {{smaller|It includes ''Mysidæ, Squillidæ,'' the opossum-shrimps, mantis-shrimps, etc. See cut under ''Squillidæ.''}}

