'''anomy†''' (an′ọ̄-mi), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἀνομία, lawlessness, < ἄνομος, lawless: see ''anomo-''.] A violation of law, especially of divine law; lawlessness.
{{smaller block|The delights of the body betray us, through our over-indulgence to them, and lead us captive to ''anomy'' and disobedience.{{right|''Glanville.''}}}}

'''anon''' (a-non′), ''adv.'' and ''interj.'', orig. ''prep. phr.'' [< ME. ''anon'', ''anoon'', ''onon'', ''onoon'', also ''anan'', ''onan'', and with dat. term. ''anone'', ''onane'', etc. (cf. equiv. ME. ''in oon'', ''in an'' = OHG. MHG. ''in ein''), < AS. ''on ān'' (acc.), ''on āne'' (dat.), in one, together, straightway: ''on'', E. ''on''; ''ān'', E. ''one''. Cf. ''anan'', a mod. dial. form of ''anon''.] '''1†.''' In one and the same direction; straight on.—'''2.''' Straightway; forthwith; on the instant; immediately.
{{smaller block|The same is he that heareth the word, and ''anon'' with joy receiveth it.{{right|Mat. xiii. 20.}}}}
Hence, like other words of the same literal meaning, passing into—'''3.''' Quickly; soon; in a short time; by and by.
{{smaller block|Such good men as he which is ''anon'' to be interred.{{right|''C. Mather'', Mag. Chris., iv. 7}}}}
'''4.''' At another time; again; now again.
{{smaller block|Sometimes he trots, . . . ''anon'' he rears upright.{{right|''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, l. 279.}}}}
{{smaller block|The varying lights and shadows of her temper, now so mirthful, and ''anon'' so sad with mysterious gloom.{{right|''Hawthorne'', Marble Faun, ix.}}}}
'''5.''' As a response, same as ''anan''.—'''Ever and anon,''' {{smaller|from time to time; now and then; time after time.}}

